    "file_problem": "Text contains the full paper; no apparent garbling or cutoff.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests low-precision gradient communication and its effect on test accuracy, but does not test any listed version about memorization, architecture, optimizer choice, batch size, dropout, generalization measures, or memorized examples.",
    "gate_note": "The study's results concern test accuracy under low-precision gradient communication. They do not report training error alongside test error or test any listed version. Rows below capture reported accuracy results relevant to the claim's subject, but they are not evidence for a listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The study evaluates its own gradient-communication method and adopts the large-batch training recipe from Goyal et al., an original source for V5. It does not compare large and small batches for held-out performance. Its precision comparisons test none of V1–V8.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate: the authors conclude that APS reduces gradient-communication cost while preserving accuracy in several evaluated applications, and that hybrid precision matches the FP32 baseline within the same epoch budget. This does not establish any listed claim version.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The intervention is layer-wise scaling of gradients communicated in customized low-precision floating-point formats, with distributed all-reduce; the study uses natural-label image and segmentation tasks and does not study the listed claim mechanisms.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Accuracy varies with precision format, APS use, optimizer (LARS versus other reported setup), all-reduce grouping, and use of FP32 for the last classification layer.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "APS often preserves held-out accuracy with eight-bit gradient communication; accuracy depends on precision format, scaling, and last-layer precision. Hybrid training reaches 76.09% ImageNet accuracy versus 76.02% for FP32.",
        "proposed_claim": "Layer-wise gradient scaling can reduce communication precision while preserving held-out performance in distributed training.",
